WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:09.951
The movies depict Cleopatra, the last and most famous queen of Egypt, as a beautiful woman living a

2
00:00:09.952 --> 00:00:14.443
dramatic life and dying a tragic death. In reality, Cleopatra was not a beauty in the conventional sense.

3
00:00:14.444 --> 00:00:19.996
Coins minted during her reign show a woman with masculine features and a hooknose. But her life certainly

4
00:00:19.997 --> 00:00:22.273
was dramatic. She was the last pharaoh of Egypt, and two powerful Roman rulers fell in love

5
00:00:22.274 --> 00:00:29.546
with her and fought wars in her behalf. She has been portrayed as both a passionate, romantic heroine

6
00:00:29.547 --> 00:00:36.101
and an ambitious, calculating ruler. But who really was Cleopatra? First of all, Cleopatra was not Egyptian. Her

7
00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:38.881
ancestors came from the Greek kingdom of Macedon, along with the famous conqueror Alexander the Great. When Alexander

8
00:00:38.882 --> 00:00:41.532
swept across the Middle East, he brought with him thousands of soldiers and introduced Greek culture to the

9
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:47.985
areas he conquered. After Alexander’s death in 323 BC, one of his generals, a Macedonian named Ptolemy, took

10
00:00:47.986 --> 00:00:55.811
over the kingdom of Egypt. As the pharaoh, Ptolemy began a dynasty that lasted three hundred years,

11
00:00:55.812 --> 00:01:04.916
with Cleopatra as his last royal descendant. "Cleopatra" was a common name given to daughters in the royal

12
00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:12.621
family in Ptolemaic Egypt, and the woman known as Cleopatra was actually Cleopatra VII, a daughter born to

13
00:01:12.622 --> 00:01:17.347
Ptolemy XII in 69 BC. In Cleopatra’s time, Rome was expanding its boundaries while the pharaoh’s power was

14
00:01:17.348 --> 00:01:25.958
shrinking. Cleopatra’s father, Ptolemy XII, had resorted to bribing Roman officials to keep Egypt from being absorbed into

15
00:01:25.959 --> 00:01:31.896
Roman territory. In order to preserve the purity of the royal bloodline in Egypt, brothers and sisters were

16
00:01:31.897 --> 00:01:39.991
often married. This was the case for Cleopatra, who was wed to her younger brother Ptolemy XIII

17
00:01:39.992 --> 00:01:46.268
when she was around 18 and he was 10. After the death of her father in 51 BC,

18
00:01:46.269 --> 00:01:54.999
Cleopatra was the dominant ruler of Egypt for about three years, until her brother-husband and his advisors had

19
00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:05.986
her exiled. Undeterred, she raised an army to fight for her position as co-ruler of Egypt. When Julius

20
00:02:05.987 --> 00:02:11.873
Caesar, the ruler of Rome, claimed the right to act as an arbiter between Cleopatra and her brother,

21
00:02:11.874 --> 00:02:18.525
Cleopatra realized that having a good relationship with Caesar was essential to regaining her throne. So, after Caesar

22
00:02:18.526 --> 00:02:24.922
arrived in Egypt in 48 BC, she persuaded him to support her return as queen. Reportedly, she

23
00:02:24.923 --> 00:02:32.071
had her attendant smuggle her into Caesar’s palace concealed in a rolled-up carpet. When she presented herself as

24
00:02:32.072 --> 00:02:38.389
the rug was unrolled, Caesar was charmed. Even though Caesar had reinstated Ptolemy XIII as sole ruler of

25
00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:43.043
Egypt, he changed his mind after spending one evening with Cleopatra and declared her Ptolemy’s co-ruler the very

26
00:02:43.044 --> 00:02:47.828
next day. As a result of Caesar’s decision, Ptolemy XIII and his army rebelled against him, but during

27
00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:55.143
the fighting, Ptolemy XIII drowned in the Nile. At his death, Caesar did not declare Egypt a Roman

28
00:02:55.144 --> 00:03:05.152
province but instead allowed Cleopatra to remain queen of an independent Egypt. Egyptian tradition demanded that Cleopatra

29
00:03:05.153 --> 00:03:13.485
marry another younger brother to share the throne, which she did, but she and Caesar still became lovers.

30
00:03:13.486 --> 00:03:22.241
She took him on sailing tours on the Nile, accompanied by hundreds of Roman boats to display her

31
00:03:22.242 --> 00:03:29.124
power to her people. Caesar put off returning to Rome, content to spend his time with Cleopatra. In

32
00:03:29.125 --> 00:03:33.830
47 BC, she gave birth to a son, Caesarion, which means "Little Caesar." In 46 BC, Caesar did

33
00:03:33.831 --> 00:03:40.414
return to Rome, and a few months later, Cleopatra and Caesarion followed. They lived as his guests in

34
00:03:40.415 --> 00:03:47.556
the palace, and Caesar even had a statue of Cleopatra erected in a temple, angering many Romans.

35
00:03:47.557 --> 00:03:55.271
He also declared himself dictator for life and took control of the Roman government, upsetting those who wanted

36
00:03:55.272 --> 00:04:03.476
to keep Rome a republic. In 44 BC, on the ides of March, his political opponents assassinated him

37
00:04:03.477 --> 00:04:11.929
in the Senate, stabbing him 23 times. After Caesar’s murder, Cleopatra returned to her throne in Egypt. With

38
00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:16.532
no clear leader to succeed Caesar, civil war broke out in Rome. Caesar’s adopted son, Octavian, and two

39
00:04:16.533 --> 00:04:21.036
generals—Mark Lepidus and Mark Antony—won the power struggle and formed a three-man dictatorship. While Octavian ruled the western

40
00:04:21.037 --> 00:04:29.735
Roman territories, it was decided that Antony would be in charge of the eastern provinces. In 41

41
00:04:29.736 --> 00:04:36.520
BC, Antony summoned Cleopatra to meet with him in the city of Tarsus, so she sailed to him

42
00:04:36.521 --> 00:04:47.489
in her royal barge, dressed as the Egyptian goddess Isis. Antony was captivated by Cleopatra’s performance and returned

43
00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:56.443
to Egypt to spend the winter with her, leaving his wife behind in Rome. Cleopatra became pregnant, and

44
00:04:56.444 --> 00:05:01.384
in 40 BC, she gave birth to twins. During the same year, Antony’s Roman wife died of illness.

45
00:05:01.385 --> 00:05:05.608
Mark Antony returned to Rome and married Octavian’s sister, Octavia, in order to try to stabilize his

46
00:05:05.609 --> 00:05:14.530
uneasy relationship with Octavian. But when he returned to Egypt, he again fell under Cleopatra’s spell, and made

47
00:05:14.531 --> 00:05:23.872
the serious mistake of marrying her as well—without divorcing Octavia. This insulted Octavian, who began to work openly

48
00:05:23.873 --> 00:05:30.470
to turn all of Rome against Antony and Cleopatra. Octavian announced that Antony had given Roman territory to

49
00:05:30.471 --> 00:05:39.193
his children with Cleopatra, and that he wished to be buried in Egypt upon his death. Rumors spread

50
00:05:39.194 --> 00:05:42.685
about the ambition of the Egyptian queen and Antony. Arguing that they might try to conquer Rome, Octavian

51
00:05:42.686 --> 00:05:45.249
was able to persuade the Roman Senate to declare war on Egypt. At the Battle of Actium in

52
00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:49.792
31 BC, the combined fleet of Antony and Cleopatra went to battle with Octavian’s forces. At a

53
00:05:49.793 --> 00:05:57.347
crucial moment, Cleopatra fled the battle scene with her fleet, and Antony chased after her, leaving his ships

54
00:05:57.348 --> 00:06:06.731
behind to be captured or destroyed. Cleopatra and Mark Antony returned to Egypt with Octavian’s forces at their

55
00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:11.508
heels. With defeat inevitable, Cleopatra locked herself in the mausoleum that she had built. It is said that

56
00:06:11.509 --> 00:06:17.161
Antony, on hearing a false report that Cleopatra had died, committed suicide. Some scholars believe that after Mark

57
00:06:17.162 --> 00:06:23.087
Antony’s death, Cleopatra tried to seduce Octavian in order to ally herself with another powerful man, but she

58
00:06:23.088 --> 00:06:33.313
was not successful. Aware that she would be paraded through Rome as Octavian’s captive, Cleopatra committed suicide,

59
00:06:33.314 --> 00:06:41.798
supposedly by allowing an asp to bite her. The last queen of Egypt died at the age of

60
00:06:41.799 --> 00:06:48.298
39, and Egypt finally became a Roman province. Throughout Cleopatra’s reign, she understood the significance of the events

61
00:06:48.299 --> 00:06:55.528
in her world and responded to each change. She used all her charm, wit, and talents of persuasion

62
00:06:55.529 --> 00:07:01.189
to put herself in a position of power. Historians think she had a great sense of theatrical presentation

63
00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:09.542
and knew how to impress people with shows of wealth and her natural charisma. This combination of skills

64
00:07:09.543 --> 00:07:12.677
was difficult to resist. Although she did not achieve her goal of keeping Egypt independent from Rome,

65
00:07:12.678 --> 00:07:19.656
she still left an indelible mark on history. And while we may never succeed in separating the truth

66
00:07:19.657 --> 00:07:28.524
from the myth, Cleopatra lives on in the countless works of art she has inspired through the ages.

